Fig. 3 Proposed LLM workflow. a Current LLM workflow. b Proposed LLM workflow. The reviewers attempted to identify instances of inaccuracy or false logic in each diagnostic reasoning rationale, blinded to the index question, gold standard answer, or grade of the LLM response. Reviewers were blinded to the index question to simulate a clinical situation where a physician is evaluating an LLM case interpretation without examining the patient themselves. Arguments with false logic or inaccuracies were tallied and a comparison was made between rationales supporting correct ## REFERENCES

ing metrics used are mean absolute error (MAE), scaled MAE, mean absolute scaled error (MASE), symmetric mean absolute percentage error (SMAPE) and Spearman correlation coefficient defined as follows: $$
\begin{gathered}
M A E=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}}\left|v_{t}^{(i)}-\hat{v}_{t}^{(i)}\right| \\
\text { scaled } M A E=\frac{M A E}{\sigma}
\end{gathered}
$$ where $\sigma$ is the standard deviation of the clinical variable after outlier filtering; $$
\begin{gathered}
M A S E=\frac{M A E}{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}}\left|v_{t}^{(i)}-v_{0}^{(i)}\right|} \\
S M A P E=\frac{200}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}} \frac{\left|v_{t}^{(i)}-\hat{v}_{t}^{(i)}\right|}{\left|v_{t}^{(i)}\right|+\left|\hat{v}_{t}^{(i)}\right|} \mathbb{1}_{\left\{\left|\mathrm{v}_{\mathrm{t}}^{(i)}\right|+\left|\hat{v}_{\mathrm{t}}^{(i)}\right| \neq 0\right\}}
\end{gathered}
$$ where $\mathbb{1}$ is the indicator function to avoid division by 0;

hbb{1}_{\left\{\left|\mathrm{v}_{\mathrm{t}}^{(i)}\right|+\left|\hat{v}_{\mathrm{t}}^{(i)}\right| \neq 0\right\}}
\end{gathered}
$$ where $\mathbb{1}$ is the indicator function to avoid division by 0; $$
\text { Spearman } \rho=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}}\left(R\left[v_{t}^{(i)}\right]-\underline{R[v])}\right)\left(R\left[\hat{v}_{t}^{(i)}\right]-\underline{R[\widehat{v}]}\right)}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}}\left(R\left[v_{t}^{(i)}\right]-\underline{R[v]}\right)^{2} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}}\left(R\left[\widehat{v}_{t}^{(i)}\right]-\underline{R[\widehat{v}]}\right)^{2}}}
$$ where $R[$.$] is a rank function, ordering values from lowest to the highest,$ whereby, for the data points with the same value, their average rank is assigned, and $R[v]=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}} R\left[v_{t}^{(i)}\right]$ and $R[\widehat{v}]=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{T_{i}} \sum_{t=1}^{T_{i}} R\left[\widehat{v}_{t}^{(i)}\right]$ are the mean ranks of actual and predicted values, respectively.

f it is "normal" or "low", and as "not normal" if it is "low" or "high". Analogously, we label each predicted variable value $\hat{v}_{t}^{(i)}$. With this, we are in the classification task settings. For the binary classification tasks "low" versus "not low", "high" versus "not high", and "normal" versus "not normal", we calculate area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC ROC) and denote it as $A U C_{\text {low }}, A U C_{\text {high }}$ and $A U C_{\text {normal }}$, respectively. For the multiclass classification task "low" versus "normal" versus "high", we calculate weighted AUC ROC, denoted by AUC weighted (Eq. (6)), that is given by $$
\mathrm{AUC}_{\text {weighted }}=\frac{\left(\mathrm{AUC}_{\text {low }} \times \# \text { low }\right)+\left(\mathrm{AUC}_{\text {normal }} \times \# \text { normal }\right)+\left(\mathrm{AUC}_{\text {high }} \times \# \text { high }\right)}{\# \text { low }+ \text { \#normal }+\# \text { high }}
